sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch14-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The prevalence of Fibonacci parastichy numbers arises because in the great majority of fir-cones and other phyllotactic systems there are more than two but fewer than three leaves in one turn of the genetic spiral (t = 2); other observed series correspond to t = 3, 4, 5.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Most phyllotaxis has between two and three leaves per turn of the genetic spiral";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "t = 2 (more than two, fewer than three leaves per turn of the genetic spiral) \\Rightarrow parastichy pairs from 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, \\dots; t = 3, 4, 5 give other series";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XIV, “On Leaf-Arrangement, or Phyllotaxis”; topic “Tait's explanation of the numbers”. Scope: spiral phyllotaxis in plants"
}
